**Root cause:** On line 253, the expression is:

```java
if (!error && user.getUsername().indexOf('<') > -1 || user.getUsername().indexOf('>') > -1) {
```

In Java, `&&` binds tighter than `||`. So this parses as:

```java
if ((!error && user.getUsername().indexOf('<') > -1) || (user.getUsername().indexOf('>') > -1)) {
```

The problem: the `||` branch (`indexOf('>') > -1`) is **not** guarded by `!error`. So even when a prior check already set `error = true`, if the username happens to contain `>`, this expression still evaluates to `true` and **overwrites** the earlier error message with `"User.usernameInvalidChars"`, clobbering the intended error.

The fix is to wrap the whole character check with parentheses so both checks are guarded:

```java
if (!error && (user.getUsername().indexOf('<') > -1 || user.getUsername().indexOf('>') > -1)) {
```

